/-! # Equations of maximal projective order at a near blowup point

Source: Hironaka, Certain numerical characters of singularities (1970), Theorem III,
pp.155--156, section 13, p.168, and Proposition 21 and its concluding proof, pp.181,187
(blueprint: lem-trf-near-blowup-order-generators).

The fibre is the published `Ideal.normalConeFibre`, with the residue field on the left.
The homogeneous prime above the point is specified on homogeneous representatives;
local order in the conclusion is measured in ordinary prime localization.
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u v

open CategoryTheory

namespace Hironaka

/-- The canonical evaluation of base coefficients in the residue field of a blowup point:
R → Γ(Spec R) → Γ(Bl_I Spec R) → κ(y).
Source: Hironaka (1970), section 2, pp.153--154, residue-field convention
(blueprint: lem-trf-near-blowup-order-generators). -/
noncomputable def blowupPointEvaluation {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    (I : Ideal R) (y : AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I) :
    R →+* (AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I).residueField y :=
  ((AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.ΓSpecIso (CommRingCat.of R)).inv ≫
    (AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup.toSpec I).appTop ≫
      (AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I).evaluation ⊤ y trivial).hom

/-- The canonical k=R/m action on κ(y) for a point over the closed point.
The input says exactly that the evaluation of every element of m vanishes.
Source: Hironaka (1970), section 2, pp.153--154
(blueprint: lem-trf-near-blowup-order-generators). -/
@[instance_reducible]
noncomputable def blowupPointResidueAlgebra {R : Type u} [CommRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (I : Ideal R) (y : AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I)
    (hy : IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R ≤ RingHom.ker (blowupPointEvaluation I y)) :
    Algebra (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)
      ((AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I).residueField y) :=
  (Ideal.Quotient.lift (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) (blowupPointEvaluation I y) hy).toAlgebra

/-- The ideal of the normal-cone fibre is generated by homogeneous equations whose
ordinary local order at the prime above a near point is at least their degree.
The condition `hq` is the canonical fibre identification: the homogeneous class of
`a ∈ I^n` belongs to the point prime precisely when the Rees monomial `a T^n` does.
The residue-field span is the degree-n piece of the published normal-cone fibre.

Source: Hironaka (1970), Theorem III, pp.155--156, section 13, p.168,
equation (14.3), p.170, and Proposition 21(ii), pp.181,187
(blueprint: lem-trf-near-blowup-order-generators).

Proof (the node's nine steps):
1. Present R as a local quotient of a localized polynomial F-algebra T. Polynomial
regularity makes T regular local. The regular-local quotient kernel parameters lemma
chooses parameters x_0,...,x_r,Y with the inverse image P of I generated by the x's.
There is an x: otherwise I=0 and the Rees Proj has no point.
2. Apply lem-trf-normal-flat-double-initial-ideal. Normal flatness identifies the tangent
kernel with C k[X,Y], where C is the kernel of the normal-fibre presentation k[X] → S.
3. Use prop-nagata-affine-blowup-chart-cover to put y in an x_0 chart. The ambient
T[t_i]/(x_0 t_i-x_i) is regular by lem-trf-parameter-blowup-algebra-regular. Localizing
its surjection to the blowup chart gives a regular local presentation T' → R_y with
residue field κ(y). Modulo the ambient maximal ideal the chart is k[t] at its actual
prime p; no rational-point assumption is made.
4. Apply lem-trf-permissible-blowup-standard-degrees: the assumed equality Λ^δ h_y=h_R
implies equality of the two ν* sequences. The functions here are already cumulative.
5. Apply lem-trf-permissible-adapted-standard-basis to obtain initial generators φ_i
of positive degree d_i whose dehomogenizations have order at least d_i at p.
6. Evaluate Y=0 in the tangent-kernel equality. This polynomial retraction shows that
the φ_i generate C itself.
7. Invert X_0. The equality X_i=X_0 t_i identifies the homogeneous point prime with
the extension of p. The induced local fraction map k[t]_p → k[X]_q sends the order
condition of step 5 to φ_i/1 ∈ (q k[X]_q)^d_i.
8. Lift a basis of S_1 from the specified minimal presentation and complete it by a
basis of the kernel of k[X]_1 → S_1. Linear substitution identifies k[X] with Q[V].
Setting V=0 gives a retraction π with C=(V)+J k[X] and q_ambient=π⁻¹(q). Thus the
nonzero H_i=π(φ_i) generate J and are homogeneous of degree d_i.
9. The local fraction map induced by π carries each factor of q_ambient into q.
Apply it to a finite sum of d_i-fold products to obtain H_i/1 ∈ (q Q_q)^d_i.
Discard zero H_i. The ideal generated by all qualifying elements contains these
generators and is contained in J. This includes the case J=0 and the empty basis. -/
theorem nearBlowup_order_generators
    {F : Type v} [Field F] {R : Type u} [CommRing R]
    [Algebra F R] [Algebra.EssFiniteType F R]
    [IsReduced R] [IsNoetherianRing R] [IsLocalRing R]
    (I : Ideal R) [I.IsPrime] (hI : I ≤ IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R)
    [IsRegularLocalRing (R ⧸ I)]
    (hcomponents : ∀ p ∈ minimalPrimes R, ¬ I ≤ p)
    [Module.Flat (R ⧸ I) (Ideal.associatedGraded I)]
    (y : AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I)
    (hy : IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R ≤ RingHom.ker (blowupPointEvaluation I y))
    (hnear :
      (AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertSamuel.summation^[Cardinal.toNat
        (@Algebra.trdeg (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)
          ((AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I).residueField y) _ _
          (blowupPointResidueAlgebra I y hy))])
        (AlgebraicGeometry.HilbertSamuel.localFunction (AlgebraicGeometry.AffineBlowup I) y) =
      fun n : ℕ ↦ (Module.length R
        (R ⧸ (IsLocalRing.maximalIdeal R) ^ (n + 1))).toNat)
    (e : ℕ)
    (α : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) →ₐ[IsLocalRing.ResidueField R]
      Ideal.normalConeFibre I hI)
    (hα : Function.Surjective α)
    (hgraded : ∀ (d : ℕ) (H : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)),
      H.IsHomogeneous d → α H ∈ Submodule.span (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)
        (Set.range (Ideal.normalConeFibreClass I hI d)))
    (hlinear : ∀ s ∈ Submodule.span (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R)
        (Set.range (Ideal.normalConeFibreClass I hI 1)),
      ∃! H : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R),
        H.IsHomogeneous 1 ∧ α H = s)
    (q : Ideal (MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))) [q.IsPrime]
    (hqhom : ∀ H ∈ q, ∀ d : ℕ, MvPolynomial.homogeneousComponent d H ∈ q)
    (hq : ∀ (d : ℕ) (H : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
        (a : (I ^ d : Ideal R)), H.IsHomogeneous d →
      α H = Ideal.normalConeFibreClass I hI d a →
      (H ∈ q ↔ AlgebraicGeometry.reesMonomial I a.val a.property ∈ y.asHomogeneousIdeal)) :
    RingHom.ker α.toRingHom = Ideal.span
      {H : MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R) |
        α H = 0 ∧ ∃ d : ℕ, 0 < d ∧ H.IsHomogeneous d ∧
          algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
            (Localization.AtPrime q) H ∈
            (q.map (algebraMap (MvPolynomial (Fin e) (IsLocalRing.ResidueField R))
              (Localization.AtPrime q))) ^ d} := by
  sorry

end Hironaka
